1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 c6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nf3 Nf6 6. Bg5 Be7 7. e3 Ne4 $5 {I have no "book" knowledge on this position and still can't decide if Black's last is good.} 8. Bxe7 Qxe7 9. Qc2 O-O 10. Bd3 Re8 11. O-O Nd7 12. Rae1 ({I feared the minority attack with} 12. Rab1 {move but White has a good plan.}) 12... Nf8 13. Bxe4 $1 dxe4 14. Nd2 f5 15. Kh1 {After the game we could not agree on the merits or otherwise of this move.} ({I felt that it was overcautious and that} 15. f3 {immediately was best but he was intent on meeting a capture on f3 with gxf3 and thus wanted his king off the g-file.}) 15... b6 $1 16. f3 Ba6 17. Rf2 Bd3 18. Qb3+ Kh8 $1 {I plan to show that rooks on the 7th are not everything. Notice how important to the position Black's bishop is, a source of strength and weakness. It exercises influence over the f-file but totally lacks mobility .} 19. fxe4 fxe4 20. Rf7 $6 ({After the game Littlewood admitted he had overrated this move and suggested} 20. Qf7 {instead.}) 20... Qh4 $1 21. g3 Qh3 22. Nd1 Ne6 23. Nf2 {Here White offered a draw but the position is hardly sterile!} Qh5 24. Rd7 Rf8 $1 25. Nxd3 exd3 26. Qd1 ({Correctly assessing} 26. Qxe6 Rf2 27. h4 Rxd2 28. Rf1 Rd1 $1 {as winning for Black. But now he loses the exchange by force anyway.}) 26... Qh3 27. Rf1 Nf4 $1 28. gxf4 Qxd7 29. Nf3 Qf5 30. Ne5 Qe4+ 31. Rf3 c5 $1 32. dxc5 Rad8 ({Having calculated before playing c5 that this move wins, I noticed too late that} 32... Rae8 33. Kg2 Rxe5 34. fxe5 d2 {wins immediately.}) 33. c6 d2 $6 (33... Rd5 {was a lot simpler.}) 34. c7 Rc8 $2 ({A costly crisis of confidence, thinking that} 34... Rd5 {could now be met by} 35. Kg1 {. But then} Rc5 $1 {is still a win.}) 35. Qxd2 Rxc7 36. Qd6 Rcc8 37. Kg2 Qc2+ 38. Kg3 Qc7 39. Qd5 Rcd8 40. Qb3 Qb7 41. Rf2 Qd5 42. Qxd5 Rxd5 43. Rc2 a5 44. a4 b5 45. b3 bxa4 46. bxa4 Rb8 47. Rc7 h6 48. Kf3 Kh7 $4 ({I was intending to play} 48... Rb4 {which wins easily enough. But looking for ways he might try to get counterplay, I decided his hope was the idea of h4, h5, Ng6+ and Rc8 to mate me after defending his pawn an h5. It's much too slow but since he can't stop me winning on the queenside I thought I'd spend a move to forestall his attack.}) ({Smiling to myself at the thought of} 48... Kg8 $4 49. Nc6 {I played ...Kh7.}) 49. Nd7 $1 {A move which has caused me many a nightmare and almost netted Littlewood $1,700. The knight threatens to go to f6 and freakishly stops both the ways my rook could cover the square.} Rxd7 50. Rxd7 Rb2 ({White is again lucky in that} 50... Rb4 {could be met by} 51. Rd4) 51. f5 $1 Kg8 52. Ra7 Rxh2 53. e4 ({The bulletin gives} 53. Ke4 {but is wrong - JS}) 53... h5 54. Rxa5 Rh1 55. Rd5 ({Paul Littlewood's version of the score has} 55. Re5 {here.}) 55... Kf7 56. a5 Ra1 57. Kf4 g6 58. fxg6+ Kxg6 59. Rg5+ Kh6 60. Rb5 Kg6 61. Rb6+ Kf7 62. a6 h4 63. Kf5 Rf1+ $2 {63...Ra5+, 63...h3 or 63...Kg7 are tablebase draws - JS} 64. Kg4 $18 Ra1 65. Kxh4 Ra4 66. Kg5 Rxe4 67. Kf5 Re1 68. Rb7+ Ke8 69. a7 {Source: 'Chess in Australia', May 1981. Contributed by Paul Summers, 21 July 2022. JS = editorial note by John Saunders.} 1-0

